+# Handoff Prompt: Continue Native Private Routing for the Holepunch Stack
+
+Copy this entire document into a new agent task. The workspace and local
+worktrees named below are on the same machine.
+
+## Role and objective
+
+You are continuing an experimental, security-sensitive private-routing project
+for the Holepunch stack under the `ayooooo123` GitHub account.
+
+The long-term goal is a drop-in-compatible family of low-level forks—starting
+with `dht-rpc` and `hyperdht`, then Hyperswarm and its testnet—in which a client
+can enable required private routing and avoid revealing its IP address to
+ordinary DHT nodes, swarm peers, or the destination peer. Traffic should travel
+through authenticated, rotated, always-relayed routes resembling an
+I2P/Veilid-style native overlay. Application and peer-stream traffic must be
+end-to-end encrypted between endpoint and destination; intermediate relays
+must not receive application plaintext.
+
+The endpoint's pinned guard necessarily sees the endpoint IP. Relays also see
+their adjacent peers, timing, direction, and fixed-cell volume. Lookup and
+announce keys may remain visible where the protocol requires them. This
+milestone does not claim protection against a global observer, complete Sybil
+resistance, or production anonymity.
+
+Do not integrate this into PearTube yet. Prove the construction in low-level
+libraries and controlled testnets first. Mobile lifecycle and BareKit support
+remain important later, but are outside the current milestone.
+
