+## Task 6 design in brief
+
+The byte-level protocol remains the exact reviewed prototype sequence:
+
+```text
+EXTEND_REQUEST_V1 -> LINK_OFFER/LINK_ACCEPT -> EXTENDED_V1 -> TAIL_READY_V1
+```
+
+The ownership seam changes:
+
+- no post-pin relay discovery;
+- no random discovery target or candidate enumeration;
+- no dependency on future Task 9 `BranchPathAuthority`;
+- the endpoint consumes already-selected Task 3 advertisement evidence;
+- the complete signed advertisement travels inside authenticated
+  `EXTEND_REQUEST_V1`;
+- only the current relay tail has an opaque, token-gated responder authority;
+- only a relay-owned dial capability may decode and contact the tuple in that
+  verified advertisement;
+- the endpoint gets no tuple, socket, DNS, bind, `trySend`, connect, or
+  arbitrary-dial authority;
+- current-tail and successor replay caches remain on their respective
+  machines;
+- local monotonic deadlines and signed wire wall-clock expiries are separate;
+- M3 adoption projects a wire expiry into the local monotonic clock exactly
+  once, then moves `{ wireExpiresAt, localDeadline, clockIdentity }` through
+  opaque capabilities without reset;
+- relay contexts may be staged before TAIL_READY only to carry authenticated
+  control traffic;
+- no endpoint next-tail/application-send authority, final-exit activation, or
+  directory commit is published before valid TAIL_READY;
+- endpoint-local cleanup is immediate;
+- already-installed remote state is destroyed on observed context/link close
+  or proactively by its local projected expiry—there is no impossible claim of
+  synchronous cross-machine cleanup without an ACK; and
+- after extension index 2, an opaque dual-clock final-exit handoff transfers
+  into Task 7 in the same runtime.
+
+Read the amendment for the exact internal capability and transfer APIs. Do not
+replace it with this summary.
